@namespace NoBorders.Components.Screens
@using NoBorders.Services
@inject AppStateService State
@* Phase 8.9: the mock's inline window-control glyphs (— ▢ ✕, design-handoff
   §5a and others), previously dropped everywhere per Adaptation Decision #1
   ("MainForm keeps its native title bar... the mock's window-control glyphs
   and the divider... are intentionally dropped here") — superseded by an
   explicit user request to match the mock instead. MainForm now draws a
   genuinely frameless custom title bar: it clears WS_CAPTION entirely
   (CreateParams override in program.cs), so there's no native caption left
   to occupy that strip at all — the parent header row is simply the top of
   the client area, drawn by Blazor like everything else.

   Phase 9: an earlier version of this kept WS_CAPTION and used
   WM_NCCALCSIZE to visually paper over it instead of removing it. That
   wasn't enough — Windows still computed real (invisible) native min/max/
   close hit-test geometry in the top-right corner, which is what fired
   Windows 11's Snap Layout hover flyout there (a second, native set of
   buttons overlapping these ones), and DWM still owned and occasionally
   repainted the actual non-client caption background (a white-when-
   unfocused/grey-when-focused strip) since nothing had actually removed it.
   Clearing WS_CAPTION for real fixes both.

   Dragging that strip is NOT the classic WM_NCHITTEST-reports-HTCAPTION
   recipe — confirmed live that message never reaches MainForm once
   BlazorWebView covers the reclaimed area (its own WebView2 child HWND
   fields it first, never forwarding it up). Each parent header instead
   wires a real @@onmousedown to State.BeginWindowDrag(), which WebView2
   DOES deliver normally (ordinary client-area input), and MainForm's
   handler explicitly sends the OS the WM_NCLBUTTONDOWN(HTCAPTION) message
   it would have generated itself had hit-testing worked — same real,
   native drag-move/Aero-Snap/restore-from-maximized behavior, just
   triggered explicitly instead of passively. See MainForm.BeginWindowDrag's
   own doc comment for the full mechanism. This component's three buttons
   stop that mousedown from bubbling to the header (below) so pressing them
   doesn't ALSO start a drag on the same click.

   Double-click-to-maximize (also part of a normal title bar, per the
   mock's own "double-click toggles maximize") was tried the same way
   (@@ondblclick on the header calling ToggleMaximizeWindow) and dropped —
   confirmed live it doesn't fire reliably, apparently because each click's
   mousedown already kicked off BeginWindowDrag's own native (synchronous,
   blocking) drag-loop, which seems to disrupt Chromium's own click-
   sequence tracking enough that a genuine "dblclick" DOM event doesn't
   consistently follow. Not something the user actually asked for (only the
   three buttons were), so left as a known gap rather than chased further —
   the maximize button covers the same outcome reliably.

   One instance of this component is dropped into each of the three
   top-level screens' own header row (MainShell/SettingsShell/
   ActivityLogWindow) — they don't share a common layout wrapper today (each
   already independently draws its own "wordmark" styling per screen, e.g.
   MainShell's "NOBORDERS" vs SettingsShell's "SETTINGS"), so three small,
   identical insertions matches that existing convention rather than
   introducing a new shared chrome wrapper for this alone. All three forward
   to the same MainForm-level window, since there's only ever the one real
   OS window regardless of which "screen" is currently showing. *@

<div class="nb-wctl__divider"></div>
<div class="nb-wctl">
    @* @onmousedown:stopPropagation — the parent header's own @onmousedown starts a
       native window drag (BeginWindowDrag); without stopping it here, pressing any
       of these buttons would ALSO kick off a drag on the same mousedown, since it
       bubbles, which swallows the click before it ever completes as a button press. *@
    <button type="button" class="nb-wctl__btn" aria-label="Minimize" @onmousedown:stopPropagation="true" @onclick="() => State.MinimizeWindow()">—</button>
    <button type="button" class="nb-wctl__btn" aria-label="@(State.IsWindowMaximized ? "Restore" : "Maximize")" @onmousedown:stopPropagation="true" @onclick="() => State.ToggleMaximizeWindow()">@(State.IsWindowMaximized ? "❐" : "▢")</button>
    <button type="button" class="nb-wctl__btn nb-wctl__btn--close" aria-label="Close" @onmousedown:stopPropagation="true" @onclick="() => State.CloseWindow()">✕</button>
</div>
